<!--
  Device HAL manifest (VINTF) for the Meizu M2 Note on LineageOS 20.0.

  Why this file exists.  Without a DEVICE_MANIFEST_FILE there is no
  /vendor/etc/vintf/manifest.xml and hwservicemanager answers getTransport()
  with EMPTY for every vendor HAL.  Most clients survive that: system/libhidl's
  getRawServiceInternal() only warns on an undeclared interface and still tries
  hwbinder, then passthrough.

  Wi-Fi is the exception.  frameworks/opt/net/wifi HalDeviceManager decides
  whether a vendor HAL exists at all by asking the manifest directly:

      isSupportedInternal():
          IServiceManager.getTransport(IWifi.kInterfaceName, "default")
              != Transport.EMPTY

  With no manifest that returns EMPTY, WifiNative logs "Vendor Hal not
  supported, ignoring start", IWifi.start() is never called, and
  wifi_load_driver() never writes "1" into /dev/wmtWifi.  wlan0 is then never
  created.

  FACT specific to this handset: Wi-Fi DOES work on it.  The 2026-06-18 capture
  v194_15.1_ais_full_scan shows "wlan0" in identity.txt alongside
  sys.boot_completed=1, plus a full dumpsys wifi and a successful scan.  So the
  two entries below describe a path that has been observed live here — unlike
  the m5s tree, where the same two entries are still a hypothesis.

  FACT on the other side: the 2026-07-06 subsystem matrix recorded TRB-FULL as
  "error" with the cause "нет /vendor/etc/vintf/manifest.xml".  This file is the
  direct answer to that finding.

  Scope is deliberately narrow.  A HAL declared here but not actually served
  hangs its client (the m681 light@2.0 lesson).  The LOS 15.1 tree for this
  handset ships a 7.3 KB manifest listing a dozen HALs; that set is NOT copied,
  because on this port none of those services is wired yet (see the
  "NOT WIRED YET" block in device.mk).  Add an entry only together with runtime
  proof that the service answers.
-->
<!--
  2026-09-25, FULL TREBLE (owner's directive 2026-09-24) — the "deliberately
  narrow" rule above no longer holds, and this is why:

  PRODUCT_FULL_TREBLE turns on PRODUCT_ENFORCE_VINTF_MANIFEST
  (build/make/core/config.mk:684-693), libhidl is then built with
  -DENFORCE_VINTF_MANIFEST (system/libhidl/Android.bp:222), and
  registerAsService() refuses every HIDL service this manifest does not declare
  as hwbinder (system/libhidl/transport/ServiceManagement.cpp:851-862); the
  passthrough fallback of getService() is gone too (:770-771).  So under Treble
  a SERVED-but-undeclared HAL is as fatal as a declared-but-unserved one:
  without the composer entry SurfaceFlinger never gets a composer.  Every HIDL
  service installed by device.mk is therefore declared below, and nothing else.
  Services that ship their own VINTF fragment are NOT repeated here (the build
  merges them): keymint, wpa_supplicant, gatekeeper@1.0-service.software,
  drm@1.4-service.clearkey.

  target-level 3 = FCM of Android 9, the lowest one Android 13 carries (m95
  measured this: level 1 no longer exists, checkvintf exit 70).  Its
  optional="false" HALs are all present: audio 6.0 + audio.effect 6.0 (>= 4.0
  required), drm 1.0 + clearkey 1.4 fragment, gatekeeper (fragment),
  composer 2.1, mapper 2.0, media.omx 1.0 (service from base_vendor.mk).

  The IWifi entry stays although its service is still NOT WIRED (device.mk
  item 1): that is the pre-existing decision of this tree, kept unchanged.
-->
<manifest version="1.0" type="device" target-level="3">
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.audio</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>6.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IDevicesFactory</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.audio.effect</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>6.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IEffectsFactory</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.bluetooth</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>1.1</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IBluetoothHci</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.camera.provider</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>2.4</version>
        <interface>
            <name>ICameraProvider</name>
            <instance>legacy/0</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.drm</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>1.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>ICryptoFactory</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
        <interface>
            <name>IDrmFactory</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.gnss</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>1.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IGnss</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.graphics.allocator</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>2.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IAllocator</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.graphics.composer</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>2.1</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IComposer</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.graphics.mapper</name>
        <transport arch="32+64">passthrough</transport>
        <version>2.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IMapper</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.light</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>2.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>ILight</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.media.omx</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>1.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IOmx</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
        <interface>
            <name>IOmxStore</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.memtrack</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>1.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IMemtrack</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.sensors</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>1.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>ISensors</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.vibrator</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>1.0</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IVibrator</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.wifi</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>1.2</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IWifi</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <!--
      android.hardware.wifi.supplicant is deliberately NOT declared here
      (2026-09-24, lesson of m95 161682f).  The Android 13 wpa_supplicant of
      this tree serves only the AIDL ISupplicant (CONFIG_CTRL_IFACE_AIDL,
      external/wpa_supplicant_8/wpa_supplicant/android.config:332) and declares
      it itself in its own VINTF fragment,
      /vendor/etc/vintf/manifest/android.hardware.wifi.supplicant.xml
      (wpa_supplicant/Android.mk:1827).  The HIDL 1.1 entry that stood here
      would be a declared-but-unserved HAL (see the rule above).  The service
      itself is in rootdir/etc/init/init.m2note.wifi.rc.
    -->
</manifest>
